WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.780
Signal Engineer is about testing ideas with historical data,

2
00:00:04.780 --> 00:00:07.990
and about the ways that testing can fool you.

3
00:00:09.390 --> 00:00:15.692
Quantitative research starts with clean data: prices adjusted for splits and bonuses,

4
00:00:15.692 --> 00:00:19.696
correct dates, and a clear definition of every return.

5
00:00:19.696 --> 00:00:25.034
A daily return is today’s close divided by yesterday’s close, minus one.

6
00:00:26.434 --> 00:00:29.243
Three traps catch most first attempts.

7
00:00:29.243 --> 00:00:35.451
Look-ahead bias uses information that was not available on the date of the decision.

8
00:00:35.451 --> 00:00:40.034
Survivorship bias tests only companies that still exist today.

9
00:00:40.034 --> 00:00:44.469
And overfitting tunes the rules until history looks perfect.

10
00:00:45.869 --> 00:00:49.943
Test enough random ideas and some will look good by chance.

11
00:00:49.943 --> 00:00:52.498
At a five percent significance level,

12
00:00:52.498 --> 00:00:59.473
the chance that at least one of twenty unrelated tests looks significant is about sixty-four percent.

13
00:01:00.873 --> 00:01:03.678
So design the test before you look.

14
00:01:03.678 --> 00:01:08.647
Keep data that the rules never saw, include transaction costs,

15
00:01:08.647 --> 00:01:11.373
and compare with a fair benchmark.

16
00:01:12.773 --> 00:01:17.725
In the course you will measure returns, volatility and drawdowns,

17
00:01:17.725 --> 00:01:23.972
design out-of-sample tests in the Quant Lab, and learn to report results honestly.

18
00:01:23.972 --> 00:01:28.162
A backtest describes the past under stated assumptions.

19
00:01:28.162 --> 00:01:32.962
It is not a forecast, and this course is not investment advice.

